    "q-94818": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94818",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "In a garden, $60%$ of the flowers are roses. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that if $100$ flowers are picked at random, then $70$ of them will be roses. Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9726\\,$ | $\\,0.9732\\,$ | $\\,0.9738\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=100$ and $p=0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =60 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(100)(0.6)=60\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(100)(0.6)(1-0.6)=24.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(60,24).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=70).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=70)≈P(69.5\\le Y\\le 70.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(69.5\\le Y\\le 70.5) & =P(\\frac{69.5-60}{\\sqrt{24}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{70.5-60}{\\sqrt{24}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.94\\le Z\\le 2.14) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.14)-P(Z<1.94) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.14)-P(Z\\le 1.94) \\\\  & =Φ(2.14)-Φ(1.94) \\\\  & =0.9838-0.9738 \\\\  & =0.010\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=70)≈0.010.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0102.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
