    "q-94820": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94820",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "In a music program at a college, an average of $45%$ of graduating students go on to pursue a career in music. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that in a graduating class of $200$ students, at least $110$ students will go on to pursue a career in music. Give your final answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9960\\,$ | $\\,0.9961\\,$ | $\\,0.9962\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9970\\,$ | $\\,0.9971\\,$ | $\\,0.9972\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.8\\,$ | $\\,0.9978\\,$ | $\\,0.9979\\,$ | $\\,0.9979\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=200$ and $p=0.45.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =200\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =90 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =200\\cdot (1-0.45) \\\\  & =110 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(200)(0.45)=90\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(200)(0.45)(1-0.45)=49.5.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(90,49.5).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X\\ge 110).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 110)≈P(Y>109.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>109.5) & =1-P(Y\\le 109.5) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{109.5-90}{\\sqrt{49.5}}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le 2.77) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2.77) \\\\  & =1-0.9972 \\\\  & =0.0028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 110)≈0.003.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0028.$",
