    "q-132262": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132262",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of getting $48$ even numbers on $100$ rolls of a $6$-sided die.\n\nII. The probability that less than $4$ wireless earphones are defective from a box containing $30$ pairs of earphones if the probability of a single pair being defective is $0.15.$\n\nIII. The probability that a basketball player scores $8$ of $20$ three-point shots if the probability of scoring each shot is $0.53.$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=100$ and $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{3} \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{250}{3} \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=30$ and $p=0.15.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =30\\cdot 0.15 \\\\  & =4.5 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=20$ and $p=0.53.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20\\cdot 0.53 \\\\  & =10.6 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20\\cdot (1-0.53) \\\\  & =9.4 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
